		c. Injection Attacks
			§ Attacker tries to input/inject different data than expected and exploits the system vulnerability.
			§ SQL Injection
				§ Injects sql commands to access whole data base instead of only querying what's needed for a particular user.
				§ e.g. attacker might inject sql command to get credit card information of all the users of the bank instead of just writing his own credit card information.
				§ e.g. `OR 1=1
				§ Can be mitigated with validation checks as well as with stored procedures. (modified queries e.g. 'Call get_options' where Call is stored procedure for 'select * ')
				§ Can be mitigated by input validation, use of stored procedures, and least privileges
				§ PCI-DSS is a standard for credit card security
				
			§ Script Injection
				§ Injects script in website to display unauthorized data on website to other users and might fooling them into clicking it
			§ LDAP Injection
				§ Injecting LDAP commands to gain details about/access to internal system resources.
			§ Can be avoided with client side and server side input validation.
			
		d. Cross-sire Scripting (XSS)
			§ Takes the advantage of the trust user has for a site.
			§ Victim is user
			§ Can be avoided by code signing and stopping cookies
			§ Attacker puts Javascript code in website, which tricks other users
			§ Scripting XSS - Attacker can also put script containing session id, cookie or other info to get information on user without their knowledge.
